1
00:00:01,360 --> 00:00:05,960
When it comes to what you eat,
there's no shortage of advice.

2
00:00:08,160 --> 00:00:10,120
TikTok is overflowing with it...

3
00:00:10,160 --> 00:00:12,520
the low-carb diet, raw food,

4
00:00:12,560 --> 00:00:16,960
cabbage soup, cucumber bagel diet.
And on and on.

5
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:19,760
If you're confused, it's no wonder.

6
00:00:19,800 --> 00:00:23,360
So here's five simple tips
you can trust for healthy eating.

7
00:00:25,120 --> 00:00:28,120
OK, this one's
a bit of a game-changer.

8
00:00:31,520 --> 00:00:35,120
If you eat a treat -
say a slice of chocolate cake -

9
00:00:35,160 --> 00:00:38,640
it's much better to eat it
after a meal than on its own.

10
00:00:38,680 --> 00:00:40,160
Why?

11
00:00:40,200 --> 00:00:44,120
It's all about keeping
glucose spikes under control.

12
00:00:44,160 --> 00:00:46,400
Whenever you eat carbs,
or sweet things,

13
00:00:46,440 --> 00:00:49,320
the level of glucose
in your blood rises.

14
00:00:49,360 --> 00:00:51,400
If it rises very quickly,
and sharply,

15
00:00:51,440 --> 00:00:53,400
it will be followed by a crash.

16
00:00:53,440 --> 00:00:56,320
And that crash makes you feel tired,
irritable,

17
00:00:56,360 --> 00:00:58,080
and hungry again.

18
00:00:58,120 --> 00:01:01,120
But there are ways to keep
these spikes under control.

19
00:01:01,160 --> 00:01:04,240
Exercising after you eat is one.

20
00:01:04,280 --> 00:01:05,840
Eating foods higher in fibre -

21
00:01:05,880 --> 00:01:08,320
like vegetables or porridge oats -
is another.

22
00:01:08,360 --> 00:01:11,360
But the order you eat your food
matters too.

23
00:01:11,400 --> 00:01:13,320
If you're having something
carb-heavy -

24
00:01:13,360 --> 00:01:15,880
like white rice or pasta -
eating fat, protein,

25
00:01:15,920 --> 00:01:19,040
or vegetables before
flattens that glucose curve.

26
00:01:19,080 --> 00:01:21,480
A little bit of fish, meat,
broccoli, an egg...

27
00:01:21,520 --> 00:01:23,520
anything like that would do.

28
00:01:23,560 --> 00:01:26,080
That's because having something
in your stomach first

29
00:01:26,120 --> 00:01:28,200
slows down how quickly
the carbohydrates

30
00:01:28,240 --> 00:01:30,960
reach the small intestine
for digestion.

31
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:34,000
And it's the same for that slice
of chocolate cake.

32
00:01:37,840 --> 00:01:39,520
Why?

33
00:01:39,560 --> 00:01:42,960
Well, for a start,
it's notoriously hard to do.

34
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:47,680
Most people underestimate
their calorie intake by about 25%.

35
00:01:47,720 --> 00:01:51,440
But also because not all calories
behave in the same way,

36
00:01:51,480 --> 00:01:53,400
once inside your body.

37
00:01:53,440 --> 00:01:56,560
Foods in their natural state -
like nuts, fruit, vegetables,

38
00:01:56,600 --> 00:01:59,560
and fish, or anything
that's not been processed -

39
00:01:59,600 --> 00:02:02,640
require more energy from the body
to digest them.

40
00:02:02,680 --> 00:02:05,640
This is called
the thermic effect of food.

41
00:02:05,680 --> 00:02:08,000
And it means that
things like nuts,

42
00:02:08,040 --> 00:02:10,360
which are high in calories,
can still be a healthy option,

43
00:02:10,400 --> 00:02:14,320
as the body uses so much energy
digesting them.

44
00:02:19,080 --> 00:02:21,920
In recent years, scientists
have begun to understand

45
00:02:21,960 --> 00:02:24,000
the importance of
what's known as the

46
00:02:24,040 --> 00:02:25,360
gut microbiome.

47
00:02:25,400 --> 00:02:28,960
The gut microbiome is
everything in the digestive tract.

48
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:33,280
It's a huge ecosystem
that contains trillions of bacteria,

49
00:02:33,320 --> 00:02:35,240
fungi, viruses and cells.

50
00:02:35,280 --> 00:02:37,680
And it can weigh as much as 2 kilos.

51
00:02:37,720 --> 00:02:40,560
Research suggests the make-up
of your gut microbiome

52
00:02:40,600 --> 00:02:43,800
could have really profound effects
on your physical health,

53
00:02:43,840 --> 00:02:46,200
and potentially
on your mental health too.

54
00:02:46,240 --> 00:02:48,800
For a healthy microbiome,
and a healthy you,

55
00:02:48,840 --> 00:02:52,080
you should aim to eat a wide range
of different foods.

56
00:02:52,120 --> 00:02:54,600
Seeds, fruit, vegetables,

57
00:02:54,640 --> 00:02:58,120
the yoghurt drink kefir
and other fermented foods

58
00:02:58,160 --> 00:03:00,240
are particularly gut-friendly.

59
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:09,600
In the UK, 57% of calories we eat
come from ultra-processed foods.

60
00:03:09,640 --> 00:03:12,920
And this figure is even higher
for children.

61
00:03:12,960 --> 00:03:15,840
Ultra-processed foods include,
as you might expect,

62
00:03:15,880 --> 00:03:18,280
things like pizza, ready meals,
biscuits.

63
00:03:18,320 --> 00:03:20,800
But also some quite surprising
things,

64
00:03:20,840 --> 00:03:22,800
which tend to be thought of
as healthy -

65
00:03:22,840 --> 00:03:26,680
like most supermarket breads,
many breakfast cereals,

66
00:03:26,720 --> 00:03:29,560
and many milk and meat substitutes.

67
00:03:29,600 --> 00:03:31,880
UPF are not labelled.

68
00:03:31,920 --> 00:03:33,360
But a good a rule of thumb

69
00:03:33,400 --> 00:03:36,280
is to look for ingredients
you don't recognise.

70
00:03:36,320 --> 00:03:39,240
Things you'd never have
in your own kitchen.

71
00:03:39,280 --> 00:03:44,480
Like emulsifiers, sweeteners,
colourings and preservatives.

72
00:03:49,760 --> 00:03:52,080
Another approach
that's on the rise at the moment

73
00:03:52,120 --> 00:03:54,360
is that of personalised nutrition.

74
00:03:54,400 --> 00:03:57,160
And in 2014, two scientists,

75
00:03:57,200 --> 00:03:59,600
Eran Segal and Eran Elinav,

76
00:03:59,640 --> 00:04:03,120
found some really surprising
results from an experiment.

77
00:04:03,160 --> 00:04:06,600
The blood glucose levels
in their sample group varied widely

78
00:04:06,640 --> 00:04:08,960
after people ate the same food.

79
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:11,440
The explanation?

80
00:04:11,480 --> 00:04:14,120
Well, it could be
back to the make-up

81
00:04:14,160 --> 00:04:16,720
of our unique gut microbiomes.

82
00:04:16,760 --> 00:04:18,880
The dream of personalised nutrition

83
00:04:18,920 --> 00:04:21,320
is that people would be able
to tailor their food choices

84
00:04:21,360 --> 00:04:24,080
to the specific needs
of their bodies.

85
00:04:24,120 --> 00:04:27,400
But nutritionists are keen to
point out that some key messages

86
00:04:27,440 --> 00:04:29,480
still apply across the board.

87
00:04:29,520 --> 00:04:32,800
So, for a healthy diet,
aim for foods which are close

88
00:04:32,840 --> 00:04:33,960
to their natural state...

89
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:36,360
An orange instead of orange juice.

90
00:04:36,400 --> 00:04:39,240
Brown rice instead of white rice.

91
00:04:39,280 --> 00:04:42,360
Dark chocolate
instead of milk chocolate.

92
00:04:42,400 --> 00:04:45,520
Aim for a wide variety of foods.

93
00:04:45,560 --> 00:04:48,840
Try not to eat too many ingredients
you don't recognise.

94
00:04:48,880 --> 00:04:51,600
And remember, you are what you eat.

95
00:04:51,640 --> 00:04:53,440
But when you eat matters too.

